    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, I see where the confusion lies, my friend. My goblin analogy was meant to lighten the mood, but let's dive into the technical depths of your query. The delay you're experiencing is indeed related to the Time to Live (TTL) setting for your DNS records. When you update an A record, the new IP address is propagated to other servers around the world, and this process is governed by the TTL value. The TTL is like a magical spell that dictates how long a goblin (or in this case, a DNS record) can stay in the air before it needs to be refreshed.\n\nIf the TTL is set to a high value, the new IP address will take longer to propagate, much like a goblin taking its sweet time to spread its wings. Once the TTL expires, the new IP address will be updated across the globe, and you'll see the changes reflected for all your visitors. It's a bit like waiting for a goblin to finish its tea before it starts its day. Patience, my friend, is key in this scenario. And remember, even the most elusive goblin eventually finds its way.",
